Andrew Fabbro <andrew.fab...@gmail.com> wrote:

> In the early game, I find a difficult balance
> between starving vs.  fighting vs. healing.  It
> goes something like this:

> A. I need food, I'll go kill something and eat it.

Well, start with the first person pronoun there, and
see if you find that really to be your only choice.

NetHack is much more subtle a game than requiring
that the PC always do everything _personally_.

> B. Whew, no longer hungry, but now I need to heal
> hit points.  I'll rest/try to avoid combat.

Resting isn't a good way to restore hit points, the
monsters are still looking for you, nourishment points
are being expended to no good game-goal purpose.

Use exploration time to restore hit points, and maybe
you'll find food while you are exploring.

Running away is a very useful response to combat
opportunities, especially if you keep your pets
healthy and keep several of them around, so make it
a big part of your playing exploration strategy.

> C. Uh-oh, hungry again, better go do some more
> killing, even though my hit points haven't quite
> healed up...

There are _lots_ of other options here.

One big part of learning to play NetHack well is
learning to question your assumptions at every
choice of actions.

> Lather, rinse, repeat, get a free comprehensive
> inventory identification.

> So is this (a) something I'm not quite getting,

Of course it is [says someone whose game log is
rife, just rife, with deaths from starvations; do as
I say, not as I do].

> or (b) the fundamental tension/challenge in the
> game.

Your impression is correct, staying nourished is
indeed an essential skill, and until you learn a
pretty comprehensive set of playing tricks, it can
provide a form of game tension. Once you learn those
tactics, though, food becomes usually an
incidental issue of the early game, a non-issue of
the late game.

> Food really seems a lot bigger deal in NetHack
> than in the *bands.

Only one of many, many reasons NetHack is so popular
is that it isn't "the *bands".

Later in the game, there are plenty of monsters
whose corpses provide so much nourishment that you
can't safely eat two of them in a row; at that
point, nourishment becomes a non-issue.

> I don't really have a problem finding food (I have
> only needed to pray for food once),

Bzzzt. Praying for food should be something you do
regularly in every game you play until you are so
good you start playing conducts like "atheist" or
"foodless".

Try not to think of prayer as "for emergencies only".

Instead, deliberately don't eat food that "keeps", to
let your nourishment level get down to "weak" where
a prayer becomes useful for gaining nourishment, on
a regular basis.

> but seem to always be on a food = fighting = lower
> hit points = trend towards death cycle.

Well, then one way to conceptualize your solution is
that you need to fight less and eat more, right?

Throwing more things in that can re-balance the
equation, like judicious use of prayer, will be
another part of your eventual strategy.

> Or is it the case that I'm not descending fast
> enough?

Certainly spending your healing time _resting_
instead of doing useful things is a contributor
there, yes.

Look at it this way; there are only so many sources
of nourishment:

1) starting food stores some roles receive
2) nourishing items randomly placed in dungeon
   levels when they are generated
3) nourishing items death dropped by monsters
4) nourishing items found in shops
5) fresh, safe to eat corpses from monster kills
6) nourishment your deity grants in response to
   prayer
7) slowed expenditure of nourishment due to wearing
   a ring of slow digestion is equivalent to getting
   extra sources of nourishment, so IDing rings to
   find such a ring can be an important survival
   improvement tactic

Hey, that's a big list, make sure you've struck a
good balance among all those options!

The point with respect to rapidity of descent,
though, is that numbers 1, 2, and 4 get used up
and don't reappear while you stick to one level,
and 2 and 4 only help you if you keep exploring new
regions, so "explore fast, descend" is a good
tactic, though it may not be a good strategy in a
good strategy _mix_ of tactics.

> I try to see most of a level before dropping down.

Searching for a secret door can take tens of turns,
a huge devourer of nourishment points, so look for
alternate routes in preference to searching, unless
you cannot find a stairway down without searching.

> Do things get easier once you get to levels that
> have more stores?

Except for Mines Town, stores are often pretty
scarce in the dungeon. Descending brings you bigger,
more nourishing monsters, though, which is even more
useful than stores that might or might not even
carry food.

> Or am I fundamentally missing something about how
> to heal up hit points?

The best way to deal with the issue of healing up
hit points is to avoid melee combat like the plague
it is so you don't lose the hit points in the first
place. Fight at a distance, let your pets fight, or
lure monsters, which have really brain dead movement
algorithms, into walking into traps and dying there,
or into trap doors or holes to get off the current
level so you can deal with them later when your hit
points are topped off, or into teleport traps to get
flashed somewhere on the level away from the PC
(player character).

Also, the level of monsters, and thus how much
damage they can do to you, goes up in part with
dungeon level, but also in part with your PC's
experience level. Strike a balance that finds you
improving your PC's armor class ahead of attracting
monsters that can bash you when you have lower armor
class, by limiting how fast you raise your PC's
experience level (by avoiding dealing the killing
blows to monsters that grant you experience points).

Some leveling up is mandatory. Fast leveling up is
_not_ mandatory until you need to be XL:14 for the
quest, which doesn't sound like a part of the game
you are worrying about yet.

> Oddly, I've found the healer and monk to be the
> easiest starting classes (in terms of surviving
> the longest under my inexperienced direction),
> which is contrary to the usual "start with a
> valkyrie" advice.

The valkyrie is a good "go fast bash hard" role,
so the play style for her differs from that for
physically weaker roles.

> The healer starts with abundant healing;

The healer is also able to eat poisonous corpses,
has a stethoscope that makes keeping pets (that can
help fight monsters) alive a breeze when combined
with healing spells cast at pets. Do remember,
though, 1) that casting healing spells uses
nourishment points, so you still have to keep fed,
and 2) that drinking all your healing potions right
away gives you lots of hit points added to your
maximum, that can be used over and over again, where
drinking the potions one by one to restore lost hit
points as needed uses up those hit point gains in a
way that isn't reusable, and usually doesn't raise
the PC's hit point maximum.

[There's a viable, risky, more advanced strategy
that involves getting in one prayer for healing
before drinking all those potions, that you might
try when you are more experienced with the early
game and with prayer tactics.]

> the monk with abundant food.

The monk is a special case where something called
"the protection racket" suggests avoiding combat
to keep XL low, while racing to the Mines Town altar
to buy lots of AC improving "divine protection"
cheaply [pausing along the way only to gain abundant
gold for donating to the priest at that altar],
which, for a monk who wears armor only with penalties
is the best possible armor, can be a huge boost
toward survival.

More detailed advice:

1) There are a few corpses that are good food for
you that your pets won't eat. Pet cats and dogs
don't eat lichen corpses, for example, but they
nourish you (around 1/4th as much as a food ration,
if I recall correctly). Horses, much scarcer pets,
eat lichen corpses but don't eat meat corpses. If
horses weren't so hard to keep nourished themselves,
they'd be perfect pets, killing stuff for you to
eat.

2) There are some PC roles and races that start the
game poison resistant. These PCs can eat a _lot_ of
corpses that pets won't eat, like kobolds, that are
common in the early game, improving the food supply.
You might want to stick to these roles and races
while you improve other skills, to improve your
nourishment chances.

3) There are many strategies for killing monsters,
or letting your pets kill monsters, or letting
dungeon features kill monsters, that don't involve
your PC losing hit points, and do give your PC a
fair chance to grab a nourishing corpse anyway, even
away from pets that eat the corpse if they get to it
first.  Search for threads on pacifism or the
protection racket to see discussions of these
techniques.

4) Prayer will heal hit points if they are low
enough, or provide nourishment if you are at least
weak from hunger, regularly throughout the game, so
long as you space the prayers far enough apart, and
stay otherwise in the good graces of your deity.
Learn how that works. Use prayer to avoid eating
various long lasting food stuffs: food rations, cram
rations, K rations, lembas wafers, lichen corpses,
so that your knapsack food resources grow instead of
dwindling. Usually prayer balanced with corpse
eating will keep the PC nourished.

5) There's a side branch of the game dungeon called
Sokoban, where food is especially abundant. It can
be a good idea / viable tactic, to run down to
Sokoban, explore it mainly for building up
stockpiles of food, then run back up to finish
exploring the rest of the dungeon and in particular
Mines Town.

I hope that all helps a little.

